<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!-- Windows.Graphics.Capture needs the SDK projections, so this overrides the plain
         net10.0-windows that Directory.Build.props sets for the real projects. D47.App is
         already on this exact framework (list.md Phase 21), so nothing this spike proves
         about WGC costs the product a framework change it has not already paid for. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1416;CS1591</NoWarn>
    <AssemblyName>MirrorProbe</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>MirrorProbe</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    OpenCvSharp4.Windows.Slim, never OpenCvSharp4.Windows, and the two package names do not
    carry the reason: the ordinary Windows runtime package declares Apache-2.0 and packs an
    LGPL-2.1 FFmpeg DLL, and its build sets OPENCV_ENABLE_NONFREE ON, which compiles in SURF.
    Slim drops videoio and contrib, which is what both problems are made of, and keeps
    features2d, flann, calib3d, imgproc and imgcodecs — every module this probe uses.
    Verified in docs/spikes/screen-reading-licence-and-rules.md §1-4, and confirmed on disk:
    the resolved slim runtime package contains exactly one binary, OpenCvSharpExtern.dll, and
    no ffmpeg DLL of any version.

    The two packages are named individually rather than through the OpenCvSharp4.Windows.Slim
    metapackage the spike recommended. That metapackage resolves to the same managed assembly
    and the same single native, and on any net8.0-or-later framework it additionally pulls
    OpenCvSharp4.WpfExtensions, which pulls System.Drawing.Common and
    Microsoft.Win32.SystemEvents. All three are permissive and none would trouble the licence
    gate — they are simply three packages, one of them a WPF interop assembly, that an Avalonia
    application has no use for. Found by restoring and reading the resolved graph rather than
    by reading the recommendation.

    spike/ is outside d47.slnx, so PackageLicenceGateTests does not walk this reference and
    is not meant to. Nothing here ships. If Phase 22 later moves this package into src/, the
    gate covers it that day.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="OpenCvSharp4" Version="4.13.0.20260627" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenCvSharp4.runtime.win.slim" Version="4.13.0.20260627" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
